pr_comments:
@iSazonov - if the previous PR #19330 caused bugs then please provide the issue numbers for those bugs as I was not aware of any issues with the fix. There were some races in the tests for the feature which are difficult to avoid when doing tests that require timing of events when run in a busy CI system that may not respond as quickly as you'd normally expect. The tests initially had a tolerance of a few 100 ms but this was too low for the CI agents, so I have used longer time ranges for timing related tests. If the tests continue to be problematic, they could be disabled, but to the best of my knowledge the fundamental issue with CTRL-C not always working (when the connection was stalled) was resolved by that PR. If you think otherwise then please provide some concrete evidence in the form of a non-working test case and I will investigate.

I have implemented this PR in accordance with advice provided by Microsoft on the issue you linked in your comment on how to achieve the result and the previous PR laid the groundwork for supporting cancellation throughout the command.

@TravisEz13 - are you requesting that I as PR author perform that test or will someone within MSFT be doing that as part of the release testing process?

I didn't think this would be considered a breaking change since it provides an alias for the original parameter so scripts should continue to work. It may be breaking for users who instantiate the cmdlet class and manipulate the parameter directly though.

issue_context.body:
Rename the parameter with an alias based on this wg-powershell-cmdlets review comment

@PowerShell/wg-powershell-cmdlets reviewed this.  Based on the documentation currently, `-TimeoutSec` is for the initial connection and the definition of this parameter should not change.  We suggest renaming this parameter to -ConnectTimeoutSec and alias to `-TimeoutSec`.  For this new capability, we propose adding a new `-NetworkTimeoutSec` (default to 0 meaning infinite) to handle the case of intermittent network issues.

_Originally posted by @SteveL-MSFT in https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/issues/18005#issuecomment-1268668852_
